Cell[TextData[{
 "Now, in the paper by Restrepo, Ott and Hunt (2006), the authors generate a \
network where each node is assigned an expected in-degree ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox[
    SubscriptBox["d", "k"], "in"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "ae6b78dd-203c-4a92-927e-940ff1ebd43d"],
 " by using a function which is increasing in the magnitude of a node\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s label ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "f1df2813-0849-48fb-8ff8-efcf43357183"],
 ", while the expected out-degrees ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox[
    SubscriptBox["d", "k"], "out"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "ad592820-5726-4a7d-b251-7bfdb05417d3"],
 " of the nodes in question are assigned by randomly permuting the function \
output which assigns an expected in-degree to each node. This ensures that \
the in-degrees and out-degrees at each node are uncorrelated. The adjacency \
matrix for the network is then constructed by setting ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["A", "ij"], "=", "1"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "35375adf-852b-48cb-b5de-1b23d86f6157"],
 " for ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"i", "\[NotEqual]", "j"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "f6cb2a4b-9ef4-43cb-a4de-37b2b145e9db"],
 " with probability proportional to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox[
     SubscriptBox["d", "i"], "out"], 
    SuperscriptBox[
     SubscriptBox["d", "j"], "in"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e21b4a48-8818-40cc-8c74-b3725f4c8924"],
 ". This generates a network with assortativity zero. The authors of the \
paper then modify the network in question by rearranging links in order to \
generate assortative and disassortative networks. Note that the Erdos-R\
\[EAcute]nyi network ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["A1", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e01286e6-768c-4369-92ed-49fe1844f783"],
 " that we have generated is an example of the category of networks described \
above that has zero assortativity and zero correlation between the in-degree \
and out-degree of its nodes, with the difference being in our case that we \
have ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox[
     SubscriptBox["d", "i"], "out"], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox[
      SubscriptBox["d", "i"], "in"], "=", 
     RowBox[{
      SuperscriptBox[
       SubscriptBox["d", "j"], "out"], "=", 
      SuperscriptBox[
       SubscriptBox["d", "j"], "in"]}]}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7e0353e2-1c32-4cc8-b8aa-e8418066e556"],
 " for all ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["i", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5f60f12c-e397-41f4-8f57-acd93406ee01"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["j", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "34072b41-8c61-48de-8dbf-b00cec0e67ba"],
 ". Furthermore, by setting the parameter \[Gamma] to a positive value for \
the network ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["A2", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "1d919907-d664-4ea4-bfc9-0b5a5b815982"],
 ", we generate a network that has less links than ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["A1", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "fc331af0-0bfa-476f-956c-846bb375247b"],
 " and with positive assortativity in expectation. Similarly, by setting \
\[Gamma] to a negative value, we generate a network that has more links than \
the assortative network generated by setting ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[Gamma]", ">", "0"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3428974d-e69b-4b6f-889b-9568ed29902d"],
 ", since there are more nodes with distance greater than ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["1", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3af4d934-84ae-4608-92d9-90eb90de379b"],
 " from any given node in the network than there are nodes with distance less \
than ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["1", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "28880947-ff78-4474-847e-d133ebc3e1f0"],
 " from the node in question, and which is disassortative. Thus, in what \
follows, we generate networks of different coefficients of assortativity by \
varying ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\[Gamma]", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "986acd99-95ad-4b94-adc2-cf32e5dcdcb4"],
 " and analyse the effect of this variable on the dynamical importances of \
the nodes which result as a function of degree, just as Restrepo, Ott and \
Hunt do. The command below now generates a list \[CapitalDelta]Evn1 \
containing, for each possible node in the network, the relative change of the \
largest eigenvalue of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["A1", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "90656ee4-da1c-4367-ba8d-894ead7bbff5"],
 " when we remove a given node. In order to increase the speed of \
computation, we amend the function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"f", "[", 
    RowBox[{"A", ",", "\[Lambda]"}], "]"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e28f9876-afaa-49d0-bccd-1045d07a8bb9"],
 " above so that it calculates only the numerical change in the largest \
eigenvalue when we remove a node:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.819383559877964*^9, 3.819383627491063*^9}, {
  3.819383718908476*^9, 3.8193837280570374`*^9}, {3.819439092640689*^9, 
  3.819439187082796*^9}, {3.8194392876482286`*^9, 3.819439375667338*^9}, {
  3.819439469083959*^9, 3.8194395305473433`*^9}, {3.819439566137342*^9, 
  3.8194397007084928`*^9}, {3.8194397412592697`*^9, 3.819439845219762*^9}, {
  3.8194399074797106`*^9, 3.819440119976435*^9}, {3.81944018672207*^9, 
  3.8194403675844955`*^9}, {3.8194428666575937`*^9, 3.819442868606762*^9}, {
  3.819442918620254*^9, 3.819442920522997*^9}, {3.8194429566817884`*^9, 
  3.8194431416765213`*^9}, {3.8194432650814824`*^9, 3.819443582181094*^9}, {
  3.819625943806055*^9, 3.819625971023218*^9}, {3.81962602764182*^9, 
  3.8196260646129436`*^9}, {3.8196397249603434`*^9, 3.819639737078968*^9}, {
  3.81963976823761*^9, 3.819639793954812*^9}},
 FontSize->11,ExpressionUUID->"0a247dd7-18cb-4510-8d92-7850b1ccb03f"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["\[CapitalDelta]Evn1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"], "=", 
   RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ";", 
  RowBox[{"f", "[", 
   RowBox[{"A1", ",", 
    SubscriptBox["\[Lambda]", "1"]}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.817504964185089*^9, 3.8175049698100824`*^9}, {
   3.8177237578989997`*^9, 3.8177237617447205`*^9}, 3.817734564206751*^9, 
   3.819009663654873*^9},
 FontSize->11,
 CellLabel->
  "In[547]:=",ExpressionUUID->"908650a4-64b5-434e-a5b5-b8895d688d1e"],

Cell["\<\
Similarly, the command below now generates the corresponding list of \
dynamical importances of nodes \[CapitalDelta]Evn2 for this network:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.819383685460965*^9, 3.8193837013484573`*^9}, {
  3.819383783603384*^9, 3.819383784289547*^9}, {3.8194435272530007`*^9, 
  3.819443533730809*^9}},
 FontSize->11,ExpressionUUID->"bdd35ab3-8f56-4a89-afce-3089ee66163f"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["\[CapitalDelta]Evn2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"], "=", 
   RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ";", 
  RowBox[{"f", "[", 
   RowBox[{"A2", ",", 
    SubscriptBox["\[Lambda]", "2"]}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8177347753972397`*^9, 3.817734775505948*^9}, 
   3.819009669600379*^9},
 FontSize->11,
 CellLabel->
  "In[552]:=",ExpressionUUID->"8fd6d5c3-dd2a-465e-8cf3-70927105e182"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The two plots below now show how accurately the dynamical importance of the \
nodes in each of the networks ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["A1", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "2953d899-af75-456e-be93-4de313cb8ecb"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["A2", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "971c1358-303b-4e36-9cde-964fe3e2d144"],
 " are represented by equation ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", "5", ")"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "918b3477-f2b1-4bf9-8e82-1a360e6ed2e1"],
 " from the paper by Restrepo, Ott and Hunt (2006). The leftmost plot \
corresponds to the case of the network ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["A1", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "ad5c6ef7-ef99-4d3c-95a0-34bf44f11982"],
 ", and while we see that there is a linear relationship between equation ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", "5", ")"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "842df4be-0b91-4bbc-b839-1320c430976a"],
 " and the dynamical importance, the relative ranking of the nodes in terms \
of dynamical importance is not preserved by the approximation. This is a \
result of the fact that for small values of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "be1dcb5e-7f0f-40a3-aeba-e9fde1d88ddf"],
 ", percentage differences in total degree across nodes are larger than for \
networks with high values of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5bc0ff1f-5051-4fd2-8096-501ce50d83c7"],
 ", which translates into large differences in eigenvector centrality across \
nodes. This means that the assumption ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["v", "k"], 
    SubscriptBox["u", "k"], "<<"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "9656838e-af81-4cff-b04a-e92171a5c92e"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{" ", 
    RowBox[{"v", ".", "u"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "4871036b-69c6-4833-8613-3d6e3237cfde"],
 " for all ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->"TraditionalForm",ExpressionUUID->
  "c4c0444f-020c-4dfa-a476-22deadb3a1fa"],
 " is not satisfied, which reduces the accuracy of the approximation yielded \
by equation ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", "5", ")"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5508afa2-6b11-46ca-86d6-d7b3c0e81c0c"],
 ". To demonstrate this more clearly, suppose that we set ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", "=", "0.9"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "93069375-a46d-4eb0-9220-7e41e44b0961"],
 ". In this case, the expected out-degree of a given node for a network of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["100", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "435ee4fb-d663-4086-bc56-ff96b0d94e67"],
 " agents with no self-loops is given by ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["89.1", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "9ba48bce-85a7-4697-bbce-8af58cb85349"],
 ". Now, suppose that there is some variation in the out-degree across nodes, \
so that node ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["i", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "a93fa6a6-a562-49fe-8042-10920b66e1af"],
 " has an out-degree of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["86", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "b97053be-8505-4e72-8d4f-0e2bbdc58a27"],
 " while node ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["j", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3e992f58-207b-47e1-8cb4-1e754544e279"],
 " has an out-degree of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["94", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "d96c314f-987a-452d-98f0-5edd20220992"],
 ". In this case, the percentage difference in the out-degree across the two \
nodes is given by approximately 9%. As a result, due to the fact that the \
network is not assortative, we would not expect this difference in \
connectivity to be compounded across greater distances, so would therefore \
also expect the percentage difference in the right eigenvector centrality \
across the two nodes to be roughly 9%. On the other hand, if we set ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", "=", "0.1"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "31ca5050-19fd-45bd-a3d9-ea46e7c905bb"],
 ", the expected out-degree of a given node is given by ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["9.9", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "d183db39-f039-4e3a-920b-2d6ad814da2d"],
 ". Again, suppose that there is some similar variation in the out-degree \
across nodes, so that node ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["i", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "fee67f66-ec38-41ec-a446-848b66bd12c0"],
 " has an out-degree of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["6", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "2938e834-8a4b-4e7b-b688-fb77e4717e59"],
 " while node ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["j", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "c2103512-5d7e-412e-8ea8-da04da294632"],
 " has an out-degree of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["14", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "0c3cc29a-c94c-489c-9eee-3aa8ef063240"],
 ". In this case, the percentage difference in out-degree across the two \
nodes is given by roughly 13%, which we would expect to translate into a 13% \
difference in the right eigenvector centrality across the two nodes. Thus, in \
the second case, the potential for larger percentage differences in degree \
across nodes combined with the fact that Erdos-R\[EAcute]nyi networks have \
zero assortativity in expectation means that the assumption that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    FractionBox[
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["v", "k"], 
      SubscriptBox["u", "k"]}], 
     RowBox[{"v", ".", "u"}]], 
    RowBox[{"<<", "1"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "26f3afc0-c7f2-480d-85b7-83824bd908b0"],
 " for all ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->"TraditionalForm",ExpressionUUID->
  "3b9deb7a-e33a-44a1-bb70-5aa9f37aed6f"],
 " is less likely to hold (especially given the relatively small size of the \
networks that we are analysing), meaning that equation ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", "5", ")"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "6b9b2001-17af-4bc7-a0f9-f7905beab813"],
 " fails to maintain the ordering of the nodes in terms of their dynamical \
importance."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->CompressedData["
1:eJxTTMoPSmViYGCQAGIQfe6S6HnrkjeOGS/XXQfRR7JmNjwF0itORjWC6FkL
